  <h2>About This Project</h2>
  <p>
    This research project at the Chiba Lab focuses on integrating multiple streams of biometric data (heart rate variability, electrodermal activity, movement patterns) to characterize behavioral regulation and developmental patterns in young children. The work bridges physiological measurement with behavioral observation to understand how the autonomic nervous system shapes learning and development.
  </p>

  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <p>
    The project involves deploying wearable biosensors in naturalistic classroom environments, synchronizing multimodal data streams, applying signal processing pipelines to extract meaningful features, and using statistical modeling to relate physiological patterns to behavioral outcomes. Special attention is paid to handling the unique challenges of pediatric data collection.
  </p>

  <h2>Impact</h2>
  <p>
    This work contributes to understanding individual differences in physiological regulation during early childhood and how these patterns relate to learning outcomes. The findings have implications for identifying children who may benefit from additional support and for designing developmentally appropriate learning environments.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
